WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:02.902
-Our first guest tonight is an
Emmy-winning actor and director

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:04.070
you know from his work
on shows

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:05.604
such as "Ozark,"
"Arrested Development,"

00:00:05.604 --> 00:00:07.306
as well as
his podcast "SmartLess."

00:00:07.306 --> 00:00:10.009
He stars in "Air," which will
be in theaters April 5th.

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:11.044
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.044 --> 00:00:13.979
-51% of a shoe
has to be white.

00:00:13.979 --> 00:00:17.383
NBA is extremely strict
about it. They'll fine him.

00:00:17.383 --> 00:00:20.320
-They'll fine him?
-$5,000 a game.

00:00:22.655 --> 00:00:24.457
We're [bleep].

00:00:29.194 --> 00:00:31.297
-What about more red?

00:00:33.532 --> 00:00:36.836
A lot more red.

00:00:36.836 --> 00:00:40.039
And what about if we
just pay the fines?

00:00:40.039 --> 00:00:41.708
Make a commercial out of it?

00:00:41.708 --> 00:00:43.509
You know,
it'll give us headlines.

00:00:43.509 --> 00:00:46.879
They're going to fine Michael
Jordan for being too colorful.

00:00:46.879 --> 00:00:49.515
-Please welcome to the show
Jason Bateman, everybody.

00:00:49.515 --> 00:00:50.950
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:50.950 --> 00:01:08.634 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:08.634 --> 00:01:10.904
Welcome to the show, my friend.

00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:12.137
-Seth.

00:01:12.137 --> 00:01:13.272
-It's very nice
to have you here.

00:01:13.272 --> 00:01:14.974
-It's been a little too long.

00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:17.276
-It's been a little bit
too long since I last saw you.

00:01:17.276 --> 00:01:19.845
You know, I think maybe
the last time I saw you --

00:01:19.845 --> 00:01:21.347
I've seen you since --
but the last time I saw you

00:01:21.347 --> 00:01:25.485
in this physical building was
when you hosted "SNL" in 2005.

00:01:25.485 --> 00:01:27.353
-Yes.
-You've come back since,

00:01:27.353 --> 00:01:28.587
but after I left.

00:01:28.587 --> 00:01:29.789
-Yes.
-Yes.

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:31.257
It was not as much fun
without me.

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:33.192
-I said "I'm not coming back
till he goes."

00:01:33.192 --> 00:01:34.561
-Okay.
-You know?

00:01:34.561 --> 00:01:36.028
They accommodated.
-So it was the opposite

00:01:36.028 --> 00:01:37.230
of what I thought it was?

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:38.531
I thought it was hard
for you to come back.

00:01:38.531 --> 00:01:41.300
It was super easy.
-The truth is hurtful.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.669
Now, what's going on here at
12:30?

00:01:43.669 --> 00:01:45.838
There was
an F-bomb in the clip.

00:01:45.838 --> 00:01:47.840
-Yeah.
-I heard you drop an S-bomb

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:49.709
in the "Closer Look."
-Yeah.

00:01:49.709 --> 00:01:52.411
-Do those go unbleeped?

00:01:52.411 --> 00:01:53.713
-It turns out the censor

00:01:53.713 --> 00:01:55.415
always falls asleep
right before it starts.

00:01:55.415 --> 00:01:56.782
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.782 --> 00:01:58.718
-So you guys can just run amok?

00:01:58.718 --> 00:02:01.154
-We run [bleep].
I'll just say it.

00:02:01.154 --> 00:02:02.922
There you go.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:03.956
I don't care.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:03.956 --> 00:02:05.057
-They love it.
-What are they gonna do?

00:02:05.057 --> 00:02:07.694
-They love it.
-They love it. Look.

00:02:07.694 --> 00:02:09.495
It does get beeped. Bleeped.

00:02:09.495 --> 00:02:10.630
I've been told it's bleep.
-I've got my 11-year-old

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:12.365
daughter here, so let's mind it.

00:02:12.365 --> 00:02:15.901
-Yeah. I'm sorry.
-No, she --

00:02:15.901 --> 00:02:16.936
-But she must understand it,
right?

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:18.638
-She can drop a pretty hot
F-bomb.

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:19.739
[ Laughter ]

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:21.040
-Well, she has
an older sister, right?

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:23.176
So if you've got
a 16-year-old sister

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:25.611
and you're 11,
you've heard the F-bombs.

00:02:25.611 --> 00:02:26.946
-Yeah.
And there was like, what --

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:30.416
So your kids are...
-Seven, five and one.

00:02:30.416 --> 00:02:32.084
-Are you cursing around them?

00:02:32.084 --> 00:02:34.353
-No, but they're learning it.

00:02:34.353 --> 00:02:35.688
-You have never cursed
around them?

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:36.956
-I mean, I think I've cursed
around them,

00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:38.824
but they live near a park
in New York City.

00:02:38.824 --> 00:02:40.193
It's not like...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:41.961
-What, are they moving
dime bags out there?

00:02:41.961 --> 00:02:43.596
[ Laughter ]

00:02:43.596 --> 00:02:44.930
How are they hearing...

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:46.499
-They have internships

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:48.067
with some of the,
like, lower-end dealers.

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:49.402
-I got it.
So they're just packaging.

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:50.870
-'Cause I told them, "Don't go
out there

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.805
and just think you can, like,
take over a corner."

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:53.840
-Sure.
-Like, you know.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:55.174
-They're probably packaging

00:02:55.174 --> 00:02:56.809
and they're giving to the guy.

00:02:56.809 --> 00:02:59.445
-It helps them learn
just more responsibility.

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:01.714
Because I'll tell you this.
You can lie to me,

00:03:01.714 --> 00:03:03.816
but you cannot lie
to the guys who run that corner.

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:05.218
[ Laughter ]

00:03:05.218 --> 00:03:07.386
-Now, when you do
your disciplining,

00:03:07.386 --> 00:03:10.323
do you ever slip
and curse and say --

00:03:10.323 --> 00:03:11.824
Is there even a "God damn it"?

00:03:11.824 --> 00:03:13.993
-No, I don't think there's
a God d--

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:16.662
I'm very good. The worst thing.
Did this happen to you?

00:03:16.662 --> 00:03:18.865
The worst thing is
when I get mad at my kids,

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:21.400
they laugh at me.
There's something.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.803
I think I get mad
like a cartoon.

00:03:23.803 --> 00:03:25.571
-Yeah.
-Oh, do you got her over here?

00:03:25.571 --> 00:03:27.273
-Oh, yeah. She's watching
the way it's made.

00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:28.574
-Oh, God.
So she actually came into

00:03:28.574 --> 00:03:30.410
the studio to hear my F-bombs.
I'm so sorry.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:32.444
-Exactly.

00:03:32.444 --> 00:03:34.213
-I made it very clear.

00:03:34.213 --> 00:03:35.514
When you bring children
under 13,

00:03:35.514 --> 00:03:37.583
I like them to wear
noise-canceling headphones...

00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:39.118
[ Laughter ]

00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:40.552
...for the entirety
of the interview.

00:03:40.552 --> 00:03:42.287
-That would be smart.

00:03:42.287 --> 00:03:45.358
-This is a film
about Nike and Michael Jordan.

00:03:45.358 --> 00:03:48.694
Very beautiful '80s flourishes
throughout this whole film.

00:03:48.694 --> 00:03:51.096
-To the extent there
is beauty in the '80s, yes.

00:03:51.096 --> 00:03:52.365
-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:52.365 --> 00:03:54.000
This was a decade you enjoyed.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.204
-I did well in the '80s.
But it is tacky.

00:03:58.204 --> 00:03:59.872
-Yeah.
-The '80s is tacky.

00:03:59.872 --> 00:04:02.208
There was a kitsch factor
to the '80s, right?

00:04:02.208 --> 00:04:05.578
The "Miami Vice" turquoise
and the Day-Glo.

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:08.380
-Do you think we knew it was
kitsch at the time, though?

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:10.382
-Well, clearly,
I think it's not kitsch

00:04:10.382 --> 00:04:13.152
because this is still here.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:14.753
-You've muted your colors,

00:04:14.753 --> 00:04:16.756
but otherwise this
is "Miami Vice."

00:04:16.756 --> 00:04:18.691
-Yeah, it's like I'm waiting
for the reshoots,

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:20.793
but I don't think
I get the concept of the film

00:04:20.793 --> 00:04:22.628
that's already in the theaters.

00:04:22.628 --> 00:04:24.564
So I can cut it now.
-They might want to go back.

00:04:24.564 --> 00:04:26.065
They might want to go
back and pick anything up.

00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:27.633
How were your '90s?

00:04:27.633 --> 00:04:31.270
-The '90s were quiet.
-Okay.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:33.606
-Except for my nightlife.

00:04:33.606 --> 00:04:34.607
-Oh, interesting.

00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:36.776
-Yeah, that doesn't usually
dovetail

00:04:36.776 --> 00:04:38.378
with a rise in the career
at the same time.

00:04:38.378 --> 00:04:41.047
-Yeah. No, the career usually
plummets if you're out at night.

00:04:41.047 --> 00:04:44.249
-Yeah.
-And the golf handicap goes up.

00:04:44.249 --> 00:04:45.551
-Well, there you go.
-You know?

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:47.353
-The 2000s, you hit the 2000s
hard.

00:04:47.353 --> 00:04:48.988
-2000s did alright.
-You were back.

00:04:48.988 --> 00:04:50.689
-Because I got
"Arrested Development"

00:04:50.689 --> 00:04:52.691
and that sort of hit
the reset for me.

00:04:52.691 --> 00:04:53.693
-Well, there you go.

00:04:53.693 --> 00:04:55.728
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:58.897
A nice way. A very nice --
-I got very lucky.

00:04:58.897 --> 00:05:01.000
-A nice reset, that show.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.902
-I mean, I would have done
a show half as good, you know?

00:05:03.902 --> 00:05:05.471
Like, I was just looking
for a job.

00:05:05.471 --> 00:05:07.707
-I got to be honest.
And I mean this.

00:05:07.707 --> 00:05:09.175
There weren't even
a lot of shows

00:05:09.175 --> 00:05:10.543
half as good
as "Arrested Development."

00:05:10.543 --> 00:05:11.911
That's how good
"Arrested Development" was.

00:05:11.911 --> 00:05:14.881
-Mitch Hurwitz
is a real genius, that fella.

00:05:14.881 --> 00:05:16.248
Then there's that
Will Arnett on there.

00:05:16.248 --> 00:05:18.884
But we still succeeded
despite him.

00:05:18.884 --> 00:05:22.655
-It was lovely.
-Yeah, bless his heart.

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:24.323
-It's lovely,
though that you guys,

00:05:24.323 --> 00:05:26.926
you and Will obviously
are still working together.

00:05:26.926 --> 00:05:28.060
Sean Hayes as well.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:29.829
-Yeah, we got this podcast
going.

00:05:29.829 --> 00:05:31.697
-An incredible podcast.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:31.697 --> 00:05:33.499
Incredible triumph.

00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:35.601
And one of the most
lovely things

00:05:35.601 --> 00:05:36.936
is you're very supportive
of each other,

00:05:36.936 --> 00:05:40.139
and Sean is going to be
on Broadway.

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:42.107
He's got a show
that's coming here.

00:05:42.107 --> 00:05:44.376
-Yes, It's called
"Good Night, Oscar."

00:05:44.376 --> 00:05:45.411
-I saw it in Chicago.

00:05:45.411 --> 00:05:46.746
-Yeah.
-It's incredible.

00:05:46.746 --> 00:05:48.581
-It's about a guy
that wins an Oscar

00:05:48.581 --> 00:05:50.916
and sleeps with the Oscar
like a stuffed animal?

00:05:50.916 --> 00:05:52.684
-I think you...
[ Laughter ]

00:05:52.684 --> 00:05:55.054
No, that was the screenplay
I sent you.

00:05:55.054 --> 00:05:56.322
-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:56.322 --> 00:05:58.558
Sorry.
-That you did not
give me feedback on yet.

00:05:58.558 --> 00:06:03.462
-I'd check your e-mails.
-Okay.

00:06:03.462 --> 00:06:04.864
-Are you going to go check out?
-There's going to

00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:06.765
be a pilgrimage out here
for opening night,

00:06:06.765 --> 00:06:10.169
which is April 24th, 25th,
something like that, guys.

00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:12.572
-Okay, gotcha.
-Check it out, please.

00:06:12.572 --> 00:06:14.273
-It's very exciting.
It's a wonderful show.

00:06:14.273 --> 00:06:17.276
And he's incredible in it.
-You did see it?

00:06:17.276 --> 00:06:19.812
-I did see it. I saw it
in Chicago in previews. Yeah.

00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:20.980
-Yeah.
Apparently it was good enough

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:22.415
to make it out here.
-Yeah.

00:06:22.415 --> 00:06:24.383
-I mean, they were in Chicago
and everybody was like,

00:06:24.383 --> 00:06:25.818
"This is good. We should
bring this to New York."

00:06:25.818 --> 00:06:27.753
-So I don't have to worry
about coming up

00:06:27.753 --> 00:06:29.755
with something
falsely complimentary backstage?

00:06:29.755 --> 00:06:31.557
-Interesting.
That's your concern?

00:06:31.557 --> 00:06:32.725
-Well, here's the thing.

00:06:32.725 --> 00:06:34.893
From what I understand,

00:06:34.893 --> 00:06:39.131
on Broadway,
if you are semi-recognizable

00:06:39.131 --> 00:06:41.333
or basically have a SAG card...

00:06:41.333 --> 00:06:43.168
-If you maybe had a moment
in the '80s.

00:06:43.168 --> 00:06:45.604
-Right.
And you're in the audience

00:06:45.604 --> 00:06:46.906
and you watch the show,

00:06:46.906 --> 00:06:50.509
if you don't go backstage
and say hi or something,

00:06:50.509 --> 00:06:54.113
that is tantamount
to giving it a pan, like that

00:06:54.113 --> 00:06:56.716
you hated it because apparently
the people in the show,

00:06:56.716 --> 00:06:59.184
they know if you're semi-famous,
if you're in the audience,

00:06:59.184 --> 00:07:00.385
and you're supposed to go back.

00:07:00.385 --> 00:07:01.820
I think that
that's really presumptuous.

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:04.223
Like, I don't know the people
that are in the show.

00:07:04.223 --> 00:07:07.527
It's like, "Oh, I'm famous,
you're famous. We should talk."

00:07:07.527 --> 00:07:10.262
That's cringey, right?
That's very cringey.

00:07:10.262 --> 00:07:12.698
But apparently, no, that's what
you're supposed to do.

00:07:12.698 --> 00:07:15.501
And Sean's case,
I will do it anyway.

00:07:15.501 --> 00:07:18.337
-And are you concerned that if
you don't like the show,

00:07:18.337 --> 00:07:20.206
that Sean will be able
to clock your insincerity?

00:07:20.206 --> 00:07:22.508
-Yeah, I've got skills, but I'm
not that good of an actor.

00:07:22.508 --> 00:07:23.742
[ Laughter ]

00:07:23.742 --> 00:07:24.911
-Do you want to, like, practice
on me?

00:07:24.911 --> 00:07:26.712
-Want to workshop something?

00:07:26.712 --> 00:07:29.448
-Yeah. Like, so I'll be Sean.
-Right.

00:07:29.448 --> 00:07:30.949
-And it's after the show
and I want --

00:07:30.949 --> 00:07:33.185
So your backstory here
is you really didn't like it.

00:07:33.185 --> 00:07:34.653
-Did not like the show?
-Yeah, and you thought

00:07:34.653 --> 00:07:36.188
the biggest problem
was Sean's performance.

00:07:36.188 --> 00:07:37.423
-Right, right. Here we go.

00:07:37.423 --> 00:07:38.791
-Alright, I'll open
the dressing room.

00:07:38.791 --> 00:07:40.193
Okay?
-Okay.

00:07:40.193 --> 00:07:42.395
Sean!

00:07:42.395 --> 00:07:43.930
Oh, my God.

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:46.599
[ Laughter ]

00:07:46.599 --> 00:07:49.135
Ah. You've already changed.

00:07:49.135 --> 00:07:50.970
[ Laughter ]

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:52.038
Right?
-No.

00:07:52.038 --> 00:07:53.306
-We talk about everything
but the show.

00:07:53.306 --> 00:07:54.440
-That's really good, though.

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:56.441
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:56.441 --> 00:07:57.876
"You've already changed."

00:07:57.876 --> 00:07:59.978
-You've already changed.
-"You've already changed."

00:07:59.978 --> 00:08:02.381
-Oh, you must be exhausted.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:02.381 --> 00:08:06.085
-I think the real giveaway is
that you bought yourself time

00:08:06.085 --> 00:08:08.086
by saying his name.
-Right.

00:08:08.086 --> 00:08:09.689
-Which he clearly knows
you know.

00:08:09.689 --> 00:08:11.056
-And I elongated it.
-Yes.

00:08:11.056 --> 00:08:12.524
-Seeean!
-Seeean!

00:08:12.524 --> 00:08:15.594
-As you're trying
to think up something to say.

00:08:15.594 --> 00:08:16.628
-To go back to the movie
for a second,

00:08:16.628 --> 00:08:17.730
this is, you know,
Michael Jordan.

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:19.064
This is the Bulls.
You're an L.A. guy.

00:08:19.064 --> 00:08:21.533
Were you a Laker fan
during the Jordan years?

00:08:21.533 --> 00:08:22.768
-There was a...

00:08:22.768 --> 00:08:26.339
I was pretty cheesy in the '90s.

00:08:26.339 --> 00:08:28.641
More cheesy than I am now,
let's say.

00:08:28.641 --> 00:08:31.710
And I went ahead and I bought
two seats at the Forum,

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:34.112
which is a cheesy thing to do.
I mean, not cheesy.

00:08:34.112 --> 00:08:36.248
It's just like
it's "I'm big time."

00:08:36.248 --> 00:08:39.952
-It's a little show-off.
-Yeah, I wouldn't do that today.

00:08:39.952 --> 00:08:41.253
I do like going
to sporting events,

00:08:41.253 --> 00:08:44.356
but these seats
had like your plaque,

00:08:44.356 --> 00:08:46.525
like your name underneath it.
They were called Senate seats.

00:08:46.525 --> 00:08:49.094
So you own those seats
for everything at the Forum.

00:08:49.094 --> 00:08:51.898
This is the place where
they played before the Crypto.

00:08:51.898 --> 00:08:54.566
And so I was there for every
Laker game, every Kings game,

00:08:54.566 --> 00:08:56.135
every boxing match,
every concert.

00:08:56.135 --> 00:08:59.138
I mean, it was -- I had a little
credit card for the Forum Club,

00:08:59.138 --> 00:09:02.274
the bar inside,
so that I could really cut down

00:09:02.274 --> 00:09:04.910
on the time it takes
to get inebriated.

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:08.147
You know? It was a fun decade,

00:09:08.147 --> 00:09:10.115
but not very prosperous.

00:09:10.115 --> 00:09:13.552
-And when it was over, did
they give you the chair?

00:09:13.552 --> 00:09:15.120
Did they give you
your two seats?

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:16.789
-No, they just sold it
to the next cheesy guy.

00:09:16.789 --> 00:09:18.257
[ Laughter ]

00:09:18.257 --> 00:09:20.392
-They were prying off the plaque
with a screwdriver.

00:09:20.392 --> 00:09:22.061
-You run out
of money after living

00:09:22.061 --> 00:09:23.862
like that for a while.
They take the chair back.

00:09:23.862 --> 00:09:24.863
-When they say, "Mr. Bateman,

00:09:24.863 --> 00:09:26.164
your Forum card has been
denied."

00:09:26.164 --> 00:09:27.566
-Yeah, exactly.

00:09:27.566 --> 00:09:29.468
"We'll take that back, please."

00:09:29.468 --> 00:09:30.569
But yeah, I got to see a lot.

00:09:30.569 --> 00:09:32.370
I got to see Jordan play
there quite a bit.

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:34.607
-Were you one of those L.A.
actors at the time

00:09:34.607 --> 00:09:38.110
who was playing
in the pickup basketball games?

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:40.646
-There was a little bit
of a group of those.

00:09:40.646 --> 00:09:43.816
Yeah. I was working at
Warner Bros.

00:09:43.816 --> 00:09:47.052
George Clooney had a game there
right behind the "ER" stage.

00:09:47.052 --> 00:09:48.721
-There you go.
I've always heard about that.

00:09:48.721 --> 00:09:50.489
That must have been a pretty
cool game to be a part of.

00:09:50.489 --> 00:09:51.623
-Well, I was
not a part of that.

00:09:51.623 --> 00:09:55.227
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:55.227 --> 00:09:57.062
-You were there.
You were physically nearby.

00:09:57.062 --> 00:09:59.265
-When they got thirsty,
they'd go,

00:09:59.265 --> 00:10:00.465
"And we'll take the bottle."

00:10:00.465 --> 00:10:01.801
And so I'd give the water.

00:10:01.801 --> 00:10:04.503
No, I would do
little drive-bys, you know,

00:10:04.503 --> 00:10:06.538
to see if they needed
that extra player.

00:10:06.538 --> 00:10:09.007
-Is that in sort of
Hollywood studio lot golf cart?

00:10:09.007 --> 00:10:10.009
-Exactly, yeah.

00:10:10.009 --> 00:10:11.510
With no horn.
So you kind of go,

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:15.147
"Hey, beep, beep, guys.
Anybody...?

00:10:15.147 --> 00:10:17.817
Want to...?"
[ Laughter ]

00:10:17.817 --> 00:10:20.586
No, Noah Wyle and and George

00:10:20.586 --> 00:10:22.054
were just fine playing
one-on-one.

00:10:22.054 --> 00:10:23.855
-Yeah.
I mean, you got to really be

00:10:23.855 --> 00:10:25.357
willing to suffer
through awkwardness

00:10:25.357 --> 00:10:27.626
if you let a man slowly
go by in a golf cart.

00:10:27.626 --> 00:10:28.827
[ Laughter ]

00:10:28.827 --> 00:10:30.229
-Just doing tight circles.

00:10:30.229 --> 00:10:32.597
Just doing figure eights out
in front of it.

00:10:32.597 --> 00:10:34.834
-I want to close
because you were in a sketch

00:10:34.834 --> 00:10:36.401
I think about
all the time in 2005.

00:10:36.401 --> 00:10:37.602
I'm going to bring it back
to the beginning.

00:10:37.602 --> 00:10:39.104
When we met at "SNL" --
We didn't meet,

00:10:39.104 --> 00:10:40.705
but the last time we saw
each other in this building.

00:10:40.705 --> 00:10:42.108
And it was --
Do you remember T.T. and Mario?

00:10:42.108 --> 00:10:43.108
Do you remember that sketch?

00:10:43.108 --> 00:10:45.210
-T.T. and Mario. I do not.

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:48.046
-You and Amy Poehler --
-Was I T.T. or Mario?

00:10:48.046 --> 00:10:49.215
-You were neither.
-Okay.

00:10:49.215 --> 00:10:50.583
[ Laughter ]

00:10:50.583 --> 00:10:52.484
-You guys were doing
an album ad

00:10:52.484 --> 00:10:55.254
for a very famous soul group
called T.T. and Mario,

00:10:55.254 --> 00:10:57.923
and it was like one of those
like sort of '70 record album

00:10:57.923 --> 00:10:59.425
where you're like,
"Do you remember this hit song?"

00:10:59.425 --> 00:11:01.961
And so you introduced a song,
and this is a song

00:11:01.961 --> 00:11:04.630
that was so beautiful
and moving to me --

00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:05.897
and this is a true story --

00:11:05.897 --> 00:11:07.866
my wife and I put it
on our wedding playlist.

00:11:07.866 --> 00:11:09.067
-Come on.
-Yeah.

00:11:09.067 --> 00:11:10.436
And I think for everybody
who doesn't remember,

00:11:10.436 --> 00:11:12.337
this is a really gorgeous song.
Can we just watch a clip?

00:11:12.337 --> 00:11:13.739
-Let's do it.

00:11:13.739 --> 00:11:15.007
-I know you're going to know
this one.

00:11:15.007 --> 00:11:19.645
Who didn't groove on this bad
mama jama in 1977?

00:11:19.645 --> 00:11:21.413
"Roller Skate Love."

00:11:21.413 --> 00:11:24.483 line:0%
♪♪

00:11:24.483 --> 00:11:26.518
-♪ You are so beautiful ♪

00:11:26.518 --> 00:11:29.655
-♪ And you are my knight
in shining armor ♪

00:11:29.655 --> 00:11:31.390
-♪ Let me put it in
a little bit ♪

00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:32.725
-♪ Just the tip ♪

00:11:32.725 --> 00:11:35.060
[ Laughter ]

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:36.562
-Really beautiful.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:11:36.562 --> 00:11:40.766
Really beautiful.
It was our first dance.

00:11:40.766 --> 00:11:44.003
What a joy to have you back.
It is so lovely to see you.

00:11:44.003 --> 00:11:45.303
Thanks for making
the time for us, my friend.

00:11:45.303 --> 00:11:48.407
-I'm so glad to be here.
Thank you, buddy.

00:11:48.407 --> 00:11:50.776
-That's Jason Bateman,
everybody. "Air" premieres

00:11:50.776 --> 00:11:52.344
globally tomorrow.
We'll be right back

00:11:52.344 --> 00:11:55.448
with Congresswoman
Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez.

